								 10 Best AI Video Generators (November 2026) 











													 Published  

														 November 16, 2022 



													 Updated  

														 August 7, 2026 





												 By 

													 Alex McFarland  																																						 








 Add Unite.AI to your preferred sources on  Google 




   Disclosure:  
     Unite.AI may receive compensation when you use links to products we review. This does not influence our editorial evaluations. Read our  affiliate disclosure .    

















																												 Video has become a primary format for education, marketing, sales, entertainment, and internal communication. The challenge is no longer whether organizations should use video, but how they can produce enough high-quality content without turning every project into a costly filming and editing cycle. AI video generators now cover several distinct workflows. Some turn scripts, articles, presentations, and URLs into edited videos; others generate original footage from text or reference images; and avatar platforms create presenter-led content in many languages without cameras or actors. The best choice depends on whether you need social clips, cinematic generation, localization, training content, or a repeatable business workflow. We reviewed the current product information available from every company on this list. Features and usage limits change frequently, so confirm the latest details through each product’s Visit button before subscribing. Best AI Video Generators Compared AI Tool Best For Features Pictory Repurposing articles and recordings Script and URL to video, text-based editing, captions, highlights, stock media, generative tools HeyGen Avatar videos and localization Avatar IV and V, Video Agent, 1,000+ voices, 175 languages and dialects, voice cloning, translation Topview Multi-model ads and product video AI Board, text and image to video, product avatars, UGC ads, Veo, Kling and Seedance models Vidu Consistent generative video clips Text, image and reference to video, multi-reference consistency, start-end frames, sound and motion tools AI Studios Business avatar and training videos AI avatars, dubbing, topic and document to video, interactive video, 7,000+ templates, 4K team export BasedLabs Testing multiple generative models Veo, Kling, Hailuo and Seedance models, text and image to video, editing, upscaling and effects Pipio Straightforward talking-avatar video Stock and custom avatars, voices, dubbing, translation, lip sync, templates and API Colossyan Interactive workplace learning 300+ avatars, NEO models, 120+ languages, courses, quizzes, branching, multilingual player and SCORM Fliki Narrated and faceless video 2,000+ voices, 80+ languages, idea, script, blog and PPT to video, avatars, voice cloning and AI clips Elai Scalable interactive training 80+ avatars, 75+ languages, URL and PPT to video, translation, quizzes, branching, analytics and SCORM InVideo Bonus All-round marketing and social video Prompt to video, 200+ creative models, stock media, AI agents, avatars, captions and voiceovers Synthesia Bonus 2 Enterprise video localization Up to 240+ avatars, 1,000+ voices, 160+ languages, dubbing, interactive video, collaboration and SSO 1.  Pictory Pictory is a cloud-based AI video platform designed for turning scripts, articles, URLs, and long recordings into publishable videos. Its core workflows include script-to-video, blog-to-video, automatic captions, highlight extraction, and text-based editing, making it approachable for marketers, educators, and creators who do not want to work in a traditional timeline editor. Pictory 2.0 adds generative images, video clips, and avatar video alongside extensive licensed stock media libraries. Professional plans also add custom avatars, voice cloning, advanced AI tools, and long-video summarization, while the Team plan introduces shared workspaces and brand controls. Pictory is strongest when the source material already exists and needs to be repackaged efficiently. It is less suited to frame-by-frame editing or highly art-directed filmmaking, but it can dramatically reduce the time required to create explainers, social clips, webinar highlights, and narrated content. 
 Pros and Cons 


 Excellent for converting articles, scripts, and recordings into video 
 Text-based editing is easy for non-editors to learn 
 Automatic captions, summaries, and highlight clips speed up repurposing 
 Licensed stock media, AI voices, brand kits, and generative tools are built in 




 Less granular control than a full professional editor 
 Stock-led scenes can look generic without manual refinement 
 Generative video and avatar use is limited by plan credits 


 Read Review Visit Pictory 2.  HeyGen HeyGen is an AI video platform for creating presenter-led videos, localized campaigns, sales content, explainers, and training material without a camera crew. Its current lineup includes Avatar IV and Avatar V, Video Agent for prompt-based production, photo avatars, custom Digital Twins, AI voiceovers, and a browser-based studio editor. Paid creator plans provide access to hundreds of stock video avatars, more than 1,000 AI voices, voice cloning, and support for 175 languages and dialects. HeyGen can also translate existing videos with dubbing and lip synchronization while preserving the speaker’s tone and pacing. The Creator plan targets individuals producing regular 1080p content, while Pro adds 4K export and advanced AI models. Business and Enterprise plans add collaboration, interactive video, SCORM export, centralized administration, SSO, integrations, and higher generation capacity. 
 Pros and Cons 


 Large, realistic avatar and voice library 
 Strong video translation and lip-sync localization workflow 
 Video Agent can turn a prompt into a complete first draft 
 Useful individual, team, API, and enterprise options 




 Premium avatar engines and translations consume credits quickly 
 The most advanced controls and 4K export require higher plans 
 Avatar-led videos can still need editing to avoid a repetitive presentation style 


 Read Review Visit HeyGen 3.  Topview Topview has expanded from an ecommerce ad generator into a broader AI creative workspace for images, videos, audio, avatars, and performance marketing. Its AI Board combines generation and editing tools on one canvas, while its video tools cover text-to-video, image-to-video, URL-to-video, motion control, character swapping, upscaling, and reference-driven creation. The platform provides access to multiple current video models, including options from Seedance, Kling, and Google Veo. Marketers can also use product avatars, UGC-style ad workflows, product photography tools, voiceovers, and voice cloning to build campaign assets around an existing product page or reference video. Topview is particularly useful for teams that want a single subscription for rapid experimentation across several models. The free plan is suitable for testing but includes restricted models, a watermark, and a non-commercial license; paid tiers add commercial use, higher concurrency, API access, and watermark-free exports. 
 Pros and Cons 


 Combines several leading image and video models in one workspace 
 Strong product-video, UGC ad, avatar, and URL-to-video workflows 
 AI Board supports generation and editing on a shared canvas 
 Paid plans include commercial usage and higher task concurrency 




 Credit costs vary substantially by model and settings 
 The large toolset can take time to learn 
 Unlimited modes may use lower-priority processing or model-specific limits 


 Read Review Visit Topview 4.  Vidu Vidu is a generative video platform built for text-to-video, image-to-video, reference-to-video, and start-and-end-frame workflows. Its current model family emphasizes fast generation, 1080p output, smooth motion, and multi-reference consistency, allowing creators to keep characters, objects, and visual styles more stable across a clip. In addition to general video generation, Vidu includes templates, video extension, motion controls, lip sync, AI sound effects, image generation, and tools designed for animated and cinematic styles. Reference inputs make it especially useful for concept shots, short narrative sequences, product visuals, and character-led content. Vidu uses credits, with a free allocation for trying the platform and paid subscription or top-up options for larger volumes. Generation cost varies by model, resolution, duration, audio, and whether off-peak processing is available. 
 Pros and Cons 


 Supports text, image, reference, and start-end frame inputs 
 Multi-reference tools improve character and object consistency 
 Fast generation with options up to 1080p 
 Useful templates, sound, extension, lip-sync, and motion tools 




 Credit usage changes by model, duration, resolution, and audio settings 
 Best suited to short generated clips rather than long-form editing 
 Complex motion and continuity can still require multiple generations 


 Read Review Visit Vidu 5.  AI Studios AI Studios by DeepBrain AI combines AI avatars, automated video creation, dubbing, generative media, and an online editor in one platform. Users can begin with a topic, script, document, URL, or presentation, then create a presenter-led video without filming an actor or recording a voiceover. The current paid plans include unlimited AI video projects, thousands of templates, custom avatars, lip-synced AI dubbing, and access to generative models for supporting visuals. Personal supports 1080p export and videos up to 30 minutes, while Team increases limits, adds collaboration, and supports 4K export. For organizations, AI Studios also offers interactive avatars and videos, quizzes, SCORM export, shared workspaces, bulk generation, SAML SSO, and enterprise administration. This makes it particularly well suited to training, internal communications, customer education, and multilingual business content. 
 Pros and Cons 


 Combines avatars, dubbing, generative media, and editing 
 Paid plans allow unlimited AI video projects within plan limits 
 Strong document, presentation, URL, and topic-to-video workflows 
 Team and enterprise tools support interactive training and collaboration 




 Generative media and dubbing have separate credit or minute limits 
 The most advanced collaboration and 4K features require higher plans 
 Presenter-led output is less flexible for cinematic storytelling 


 Read Review Visit AI Studios 6.  BasedLabs BasedLabs is a browser-based creative hub that gives users access to multiple AI image and video models through one interface. Its video generator supports both text prompts and image inputs, with model choices for photorealistic scenes, stylized animation, cinematic motion, and short social clips. The platform currently brings together models such as Veo, Kling, Hailuo, and Seedance alongside image generation, editing, upscaling, face tools, and ready-made effect workflows. This makes it easy to test the same concept with different models without maintaining separate accounts and workflows. BasedLabs sells credits in one-time packs, and credit consumption depends on the selected model and generation settings. It is a practical option for experimentation and short-form creative production, although users should compare model costs before scaling a high-volume workflow. 
 Pros and Cons 


 Access to multiple leading video models in one place 
 Supports text-to-video and image-to-video creation 
 Includes useful image, editing, upscaling, and effect tools 
 One-time credit packs suit occasional or project-based use 




 Output quality and controls vary between underlying models 
 Credits can be consumed quickly by premium video models 
 Less focused on structured business-video workflows than avatar platforms 


